>=20
> I've been playing with ALC260 (patch_realtek.c) following the direction=
s
> for bug #1618.
>=20
> What I've done is the following:
>=20
> 1. I did not modify alc260_base_mixer, as it seems to cover all mixers
> on the chip (i.e. 0x7-0xa, following the diagram in the datasheet); I
> assume that if it is HDA_INPUT or HDA_OUTPUT makes only the difference
> whether the mixer displays it under playback or capture.

No, HDA_INPUT and HDA_OUTPUT don't specify the direction but specify
which side of widget amp is affected by that control.  A widget may
have amps in the both sides, before the processing and after the
processing.  It may have multiple individual input amps but only one
output amp.

> 2. alc260_init_verbs is problematic for me. I did
> {0x0f, AC_VERB_SET_PIN_WIDGET_CONTROL, PIN_OUT} for all the i/o widgets=
,
> i.e. 0xf-0x16. No sound so far. PIN_OUT is defined as 0x40, which is no=
t
> a number I was able to find in the datasheet.

You surely have to find this bit in the datasheet, at least, on
HD-audio specification.

>  Can I assume that widgets
> connected to PIN_OUT are relly going to the real output.

Yes, but without HP amplifier (if any).

> I am at loss also with AC_VERB_CONNECT_SEL. What is its parameter 0x0?
> /* select line-out */ {0x15, AC_VERB_SET_CONNECT_SEL, 0x00}.?

It selects the first index if multiple widgets are connected to the
target widget.

>  Is the
> only point that I will try to connect all line IO (0xf-0x16) to 0x0-0x3
> (or more?) (which is, according to some comments in the realtek_patch.c=
:
> HP, line, mic?) I don't find any such numbers on the datasheet.

You can see the connection list in the proc file even if it's not on
the datasheet (and IIRC ALC260 datasheet has a table of connection
lists, too).

> I am sorry that I pose such questtions, but I don't really know nothing
> about ALSA internals.

Well, the above are pretty generic for HD-audio codecs and irrelevant
with ALSA internals at all ;)


